Grounded this in the actual code: `db.write()` in `db.js` is already the single chokepoint for every write in the service (transaction begins there, and only there), and `sessionUser` in `auth.js` puts `req.user` on every request. That's the natural place to anchor an audit trail — the fork is *where* the logging actually happens.

**Recommendation: Postgres triggers, not application code.** Add an `audit_log` table and a generic trigger function attached to `accounts`, `orders`, `users`, etc.:

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_id      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL,       -- INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE
  actor_id    text,
  before      jsonb,
  after       jsonb,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app_role;  -- append-only

CREATE FUNCTION audit_trigger() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, before, after)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    current_setting('app.actor_id', true),
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN COALESCE(NEW, OLD);
END $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_trigger();
-- same on orders, users, ...
```

Then `db.write()` just needs one addition — set the actor as a transaction-local session variable before the real write:

```js
async function write(text, params, actor) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actor && actor.id]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } ...
```

and every route passes `req.user.id` through (`db.write(sql, params, req.user)`), so the actor rides along with the transaction instead of being threaded into every SQL string.

**Why triggers over logging inside `db.write()` itself:** app-level logging only fires for writes that go through this code path. A future migration script, a `psql` session, or a new write helper someone adds in six months silently bypasses it. Triggers fire on the row change itself, so they can't be forgotten — the tamper-resistance is exactly what compliance is asking for. The cost is `set_config` needs threading through call sites, which is one line per route.

Two things this doesn't cover yet, worth a follow-up once you're ready to implement: correlating an audit row back to the originating HTTP request (add a `request_id` set the same way as `actor_id`, logged alongside method/path/IP at the middleware level), and retention — `audit_log` needs to be exempt from whatever deletion/rotation policy the rest of the schema has.

Since every write in this service already funnels through one chokepoint — `db.write()` in `db.js` — that's where to put the audit trail, not scattered across route handlers. Two forks matter here:

**1. Trigger-based audit, not app-level logging.** Have Postgres triggers write to an append-only `audit_log` table on every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE of an audited table, in the same transaction as the write itself. This beats logging from route handlers because it can't be skipped — a route added next month that calls `db.write()` gets audited automatically, whereas app-level logging only covers the routes someone remembered to instrument (and `accounts.js`'s `DELETE /:id` today shows how easy that is to miss). It also gives you full before/after row diffs for free via `to_jsonb(OLD)`/`to_jsonb(NEW)`, atomically consistent with the write (rollback undoes both).

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id           bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name   text NOT NULL,
  row_id       text NOT NULL,
  action       text NOT NULL CHECK (action IN ('INSERT','UPDATE','DELETE')),
  actor_id     uuid,
  actor_email  text,
  old_row      jsonb,
  new_row      jsonb,
  changed_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
-- REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app role — append-only

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, actor_email, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_id', true), '')::uuid,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_email', true), ''),
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

**2. Getting "who" to the trigger without touching every route.** None of the current call sites (`accounts.js`, `orders.js`) pass an actor into `db.write()`, and `sessionUser` in `auth.js` only sets `req.user`. Rather than changing every route's signature, capture the actor once in the middleware via `AsyncLocalStorage` and have `db.write()` stamp it into the transaction with `set_config` (transaction-scoped, so it can't leak across pooled connections):

```js
// auth.js
const actorContext = new AsyncLocalStorage();
async function sessionUser(req, res, next) {
  // ...existing lookup...
  actorContext.run({ id: rows[0].id, email: rows[0].email }, next);
}

// db.js
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const actor = actorContext.getStore();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    if (actor) {
      await client.query(
        "SELECT set_config('app.actor_id',$1,true), set_config('app.actor_email',$2,true)",
        [actor.id, actor.email]
      );
    }
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) { await client.query("ROLLBACK"); throw err; }
  finally { client.release(); }
}
```

Depth left out: writes that bypass the app pool entirely (migrations, one-off admin SQL) won't have an `app.actor_id` set and need a convention (tag as `system` or a service account); retention/tamper-evidence beyond DB grants (WORM archival) if compliance needs it; and this covers writes only, not read/view auditing, which is a separate and heavier ask.

Given the stack here (Express + node-postgres, Postgres 16, ~38k products growing slowly), I'd use Postgres's built-in full-text search rather than adding a search service — the data size doesn't justify Elasticsearch/Algolia/Meilisearch.

**Approach**: add a generated `tsvector` column weighting name above description, GIN-index it, and add a `/products/search` route using `websearch_to_tsquery` (handles multi-word "few words" input naturally) ranked with `ts_rank`.

```sql
ALTER TABLE products
  ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, query) AS rank
     FROM products, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1) query
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ query
     ORDER BY rank DESC LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

One decision that forks this: plain FTS stems words ("shoe" matches "shoes") but won't catch typos or partial/prefix input ("lapt" for "laptop"). If the product wants that, pair it with `pg_trgm` (trigram similarity index + query) from the start, since that's a schema/query shape decision, not a bolt-on later.

Left out: rank tuning, pagination past 50, whether SKU should be searchable too.

Since I can't edit anything, here's the design — grounded in what's actually in this repo.

**Key fact that shapes the design:** every write already funnels through `db.write()` in db.js:13, which is the sole place a transaction begins. That single choke point is where the audit trail belongs — not scattered across accounts.js and orders.js.

**Recommendation: Postgres triggers + a session variable for the actor, writing into the same transaction as the change.**

The fork that matters: log from the application (inside `write()`) vs. log from the database (triggers on each table). App-level logging is easier to read but relies on every future write path remembering to call it — one new route that calls `pool.query` directly, or one migration script run by hand, and compliance has a gap with no way to know it exists. Trigger-based logging is enforced by the schema itself: any INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on an audited table gets logged, full stop, regardless of which code path caused it.

Concretely:

1. **`audit_log` table**, append-only:
```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name TEXT NOT NULL,
  row_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  action TEXT NOT NULL,           -- INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE
  actor_id INT,                   -- NULL for system/migration writes
  old_values JSONB,
  new_values JSONB,
  changed_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app_role;  -- immutable once written
```

2. **A generic trigger function**, attached to `accounts`, `orders`, and any table added later:
```sql
CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, old_values, new_values)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.user_id', true), '')::int,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN row_to_json(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN row_to_json(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

3. **Getting "who" into the trigger** — the trigger only sees the row, not `req.user`. auth.js:11 already sets `req.user` on every request via `sessionUser`; `write()` needs that value without every call site in accounts.js/orders.js passing it explicitly. Use `AsyncLocalStorage` to stash `req.user` per-request, then have `write()` set it as a transaction-local Postgres variable before the real query:
```js
// db.js
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const user = actorContext.getStore();
    await client.query("SET LOCAL app.user_id = $1", [user ? user.id : null]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```
`SET LOCAL` scopes to the transaction, so it's gone by the time the connection is returned to the pool — no leakage between requests sharing a connection.

Why same-transaction: the audit row and the real change commit or roll back together (`write()` already brackets `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`), so there's never a write that "succeeded but wasn't logged" or a logged change that never happened.

Depth I'm leaving out: retention/archival policy, whether reads also need auditing (you only asked about writes), and how migrations/admin scripts populate `app.user_id` when there's no HTTP request (they'd fall back to `actor_id = NULL` and should probably set an explicit "system" marker instead).

Your service already has the right shape for this: every write funnels through `db.write()` in `db.js`, which already opens a transaction per write, and every request already gets `req.user` from the `sessionUser` middleware. Hook the audit trail into `write()` so it's structurally impossible for a write to happen without an audit row — not a discipline you rely on each route to follow.

**The three decisions that actually fork the design:**

**1. Getting the actor into `write()` without touching every route.** `write()` currently only sees `text` and `params` — no `req.user`. Threading `actor` through every call site works but is easy to forget on the next route someone adds, which defeats the "for every write" requirement. Use `AsyncLocalStorage` instead: have `sessionUser` stash `req.user` in a request-scoped context, and have `write()` read it from there.

```js
// auth.js
const { AsyncLocalStorage } = require("async_hooks");
const actorContext = new AsyncLocalStorage();

async function sessionUser(req, res, next) {
  const rows = await db.query(/* ... */);
  if (!rows.length) return res.status(401).end();
  req.user = { id: rows[0].id, email: rows[0].email, accountId: rows[0].account_id };
  actorContext.run(req.user, next);
}

module.exports = { sessionUser, actorContext };
```

**2. Capturing table/row/before-state without fragile SQL parsing.** Regex-sniffing `INSERT INTO`/`UPDATE`/`WHERE id =` out of the query text is brittle for something compliance depends on. Given there are only a handful of call sites, pass the metadata explicitly instead — a small, honest cost per call site in exchange for no parsing bugs:

```js
// db.js
async function write({ sql, params, table, rowId }) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const actor = actorContext.getStore();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const before = rowId
      ? (await client.query(`SELECT * FROM ${table} WHERE id = $1`, [rowId])).rows[0] || null
      : null;
    const res = await client.query(sql, params);
    const after = res.rows[0] || null;
    await client.query(
      `INSERT INTO audit_log (actor_id, actor_email, account_id, table_name, row_id, action, before, after)
       VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8)`,
      [actor?.id, actor?.email, actor?.accountId, table, rowId ?? after?.id, before ? (after ? "update" : "delete") : "insert", before, after]
    );
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return after;
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Callers change from `db.write(sql, params)` to `db.write({ sql, params, table: "accounts", rowId: req.params.id })`. The audit insert rides in the *same transaction* as the write itself — commit both or roll back both, so you can never end up with a write that has no audit record.

**3. Making the log tamper-resistant, not just present.** App-level insert-only discipline isn't enough for compliance if anyone with DB credentials can edit history. `REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM <app_role>` at the database level, and if the compliance bar is high, ship rows to an append-only sink (separate DB, S3 with object lock) outside the app's write path.

**Schema:**

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  occurred_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  actor_id uuid,
  actor_email text,
  account_id uuid,
  table_name text NOT NULL,
  row_id text NOT NULL,
  action text NOT NULL CHECK (action IN ('insert','update','delete')),
  before jsonb,
  after jsonb
);
CREATE INDEX ON audit_log (table_name, row_id);
CREATE INDEX ON audit_log (actor_id);
```

Depth I'm leaving out: retention/export policy, and whether denied (401/403) attempts need logging too — both are policy questions for whoever owns the compliance requirement, not architecture forks.

Read through `db.js`, `auth.js`, and the two route files. The service already has exactly one write chokepoint (`db.write()` in db.js:13-26) that opens/commits a transaction, and `req.user` is populated for every request by `sessionUser` (auth.js:5-13). That shape makes the design fairly directed.

**Recommendation:** capture the audit row inside `db.write()`, in the same transaction as the mutation, not via a separate service or DB trigger.

```js
// db.js
async function write(text, params, audit) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    if (audit) {
      await client.query(
        `INSERT INTO audit_log (actor_id, actor_email, table_name, row_id, action, before, after, request_id)
         VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8)`,
        [audit.actorId, audit.actorEmail, audit.table, res.rows[0].id, audit.action,
         audit.before || null, res.rows[0], audit.requestId]
      );
    }
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) { await client.query("ROLLBACK"); throw err; }
  finally { client.release(); }
}
```

Callers pass `{ actorId: req.user.id, actorEmail: req.user.email, table: "accounts", action: "update", requestId }`. Same transaction means the write and its audit row succeed or fail together — no dual-write gap.

One thing this exposes: the current handlers don't read before they write (accounts.js:6-12, orders.js:14-20), so there's no "before" value to log. You'd need a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` immediately before the `UPDATE` inside the same transaction to get the before-image — small change to each route, not to `write()` itself.

**The fork that matters:** app-level capture (what I sketched) vs. Postgres triggers/logical decoding (CDC). Triggers catch every write including ones that bypass the app — a migration, a psql session, an admin script — which app-level capture can't. But they don't know *who* unless the app sets a session GUC (`SET LOCAL app.user_id = ...`) per request, and this service has no such out-of-band write path today. I'd start app-level since it's cheap and you already have one write path with actor identity in hand; add a trigger as a second, coarser layer later if compliance specifically needs to catch out-of-band writes too.

**Immutability:** `REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM` the app's DB role, grant `INSERT` only. That's enough for most compliance bars; if you need tamper-evidence against someone with superuser/DB-admin access, ship audit rows to an external append-only sink (S3 with object lock, a Kafka topic) in addition to the table.

Left out: retention/redaction policy (billing_email in the `after` blob is PII), and whether reads need auditing too — you only asked about writes.

Two things make this straightforward here: every write already funnels through `db.write()` (db.js:13), and every request already has `req.user` resolved before hitting a route handler (auth.js:5). The audit trail should hook into that choke point rather than being added to each route.

**Approach**: extend `write()` to take an actor and log the change in the same transaction as the write itself — an audit row that didn't commit with its write isn't trustworthy for compliance:

```js
async function write(text, params, actor) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    const row = res.rows[0];
    await client.query(
      "INSERT INTO audit_log (actor_id, table_name, row_id, action, before, after) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5, $6)",
      [actor.id, tableFromQuery(text), row.id, actionFromQuery(text), null, row]
    );
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return row;
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Every call site (accounts.js, orders.js) passes `req.user` as the new `actor` argument. That's a signature change at every `db.write()` call, which is the real cost of this approach — but it's mechanical, and it's the only way to get "who" without guessing.

**The fork**: capture at the app layer (above) vs. a Postgres trigger on each table that writes to `audit_log` itself. The trigger catches changes made outside the app too (migrations, someone at a psql prompt), which compliance often cares about; the app layer is simpler and gives you the actor's identity directly instead of reconstructing it from `SET LOCAL app.user_id`. Given this service has no other write path into the DB, I'd start app-level and add the trigger only if direct DB access is actually a real avenue here.

**Left out**: `before` is `null` above — for UPDATE/DELETE you'd want a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` of the current row before the write to populate it, which is a second query per write; worth doing but is the next layer down, not the fork. Also not addressed: making `audit_log` genuinely append-only (revoke `UPDATE`/`DELETE` grants for the app's DB role) so the trail can't be edited after the fact — necessary for compliance to trust it, mechanical to add once the schema exists.

Read the service: Express app, single DB choke point in `db.js` — every write goes through `write()`, which already wraps each call in its own transaction, and every request already gets `req.user` resolved by `sessionUser` before hitting a route.

That choke point is the natural hook. Recommendation:

**Extend `write()` to take an actor and log into an `audit_log` table inside the same transaction as the write it's auditing** — so the audit row and the data change commit or roll back together, and nothing that goes through `write()` can produce one without the other.

```js
async function write(text, params, actor) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    const row = res.rows[0];
    await client.query(
      "INSERT INTO audit_log (actor_id, actor_email, table_name, row_id, query, after) VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6)",
      [actor.id, actor.email, res.command /* or explicit table name */, row.id, text, row]
    );
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return row;
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Two decisions actually fork this design:

**1. Where "who" comes from.** `write()` has no access to `req` today. Since there are only two call sites now, the direct fix is to add `actor` as a third parameter and pass `req.user` at each call site (`db.write(sql, params, req.user)`). If route count grows, the alternative is `AsyncLocalStorage` set up once in `sessionUser`, so every `write()` call picks up the current user ambiently without a signature change. Start with the explicit parameter — it's honest about the dependency and there's nothing to wire up wrong.

**2. Capturing "before", not just "after".** `RETURNING *` only gives you the row post-write, which is fine for inserts but loses the diff on updates and deletes (e.g., `accounts.patch` — you can't tell what `display_name` was). Getting a real before/after diff means a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the target row before the `UPDATE`/`DELETE`, inside the same transaction, and storing both `before` and `after` as `jsonb`.

Audit table shape:

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  actor_id UUID NOT NULL,
  actor_email TEXT NOT NULL,      -- denormalized so it survives user deletion
  table_name TEXT NOT NULL,
  row_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  action TEXT NOT NULL,            -- insert / update / delete
  before JSONB,
  after JSONB
);
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app_role;  -- append-only, even from the app itself
```

What this covers: every write issued through `db.write()` — currently all of `accounts.js` and `orders.js`. What it doesn't: anything that touches these tables outside that function — a migration, an admin running raw SQL, a future service sharing the DB. If compliance needs to guarantee *every* write regardless of path, that requires Postgres triggers on each table instead of (or in addition to) this, at the cost of losing easy access to the HTTP actor (`current_setting('app.actor_id')` via `SET LOCAL` per transaction is the usual workaround, and it's more moving parts). Given the codebase currently has exactly one write path, I'd start app-layer and only add triggers if a second write path shows up.

Left out: retention policy, and how compliance actually queries this (a read-only endpoint, or direct DB access).
